{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's own stated countercondition is decisive against its claim: 'If anthropogenic heat is a small fraction of the daytime storage, it cannot explain the full magnitude of the nocturnal gap.' Decades of urban energy-balance measurements confirm exactly that condition: anthropogenic heat flux is typically a minor term relative to daytime storage and net radiation, and nocturnal UHI persists strongly even in cities/seasons with minimal heating, traffic, or industry. By SRC_001's own falsification rule, that observation eliminates anthropogenic heat as the best single mechanism, yet the target still asserts it as dominant. The target also fails to distinguish the two parts of the question: it conflates 'why the city is warmer at night' with 'why the gap varies across cities,' and its cross-city proxy (population/energy) is empirically weak relative to geometry/sky-view as the actual modulator of nocturnal release.","counterexample":[{"city":"Summer Phoenix, AZ at 03:00 local time","implication":"Under SRC_001's own countercondition, this refutes the claim that anthropogenic heat is the single best explanation.","observation":"Strong nocturnal UHI persists despite near-zero space-heating demand and low overnight industrial/traffic load; the warmth is dominated by slow release of heat stored during the day in asphalt and masonry, not by concurrent anthropogenic emissions."}],"premise":"The problem asks for the single mechanism that best explains why the nocturnal UHI gap is larger in some cities than in others. SRC_001 nominates anthropogenic heat and makes that claim testable via its own counterconditions.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"000000000000","quote":"The night-time heat island is dominated by the persistent release of anthropogenic heat from buildings, vehicles, industry, and the cross-city gap scales with population density and energy consumption: cities with higher nighttime emissions retain warmer air."},{"block":"0000000